e present in `/workspace` or elsewhere. Searches for `.md`, `.txt`, `.py`, `.json`, `.yaml` under `/workspace` return only `task.json`.\n\nImplication: any specifics about how BoT retrieves/uses its meta-buffer, how Expert Prompting constructs the expert persona, how PAL invokes a Python sandbox, the canonical Game-of-24 instance sets, the MGSM language/instance split, or the Checkmate-in-One position list must be treated as **researcher-provided assumptions**, not as facts sourced from supplied documents. The design below will not invent those specifics; it will parameterize them as decisions you need to make.\n\n---\n\n## (2) Approach sketch (brief \u2014 full design withheld pending approval)\n\n**Comparison conditions.**\nMethods factor with 3 primary levels (BoT, Expert Prompting, PAL). Recommend adding CoT-as-zero-shot as a 4th level as a reference point (cheap single-query baseline); this is a discussion point, see \u00a73. Benchmarks factor with 3 levels (Game of 24, MGSM, Checkmate-in-One). Backbone LLM and decoding settings are held fixed across conditions (this is the central control \u2014 see \u00a73).\n\n**Inference-time operationalization (the central methodological choice).**\nThree nested measurements per (method, instance, trial):\n1. **Primary \u2014 end-to-end wall-clock per instance**, from the moment the system receives the input to the moment it returns a final answer. This is the user-perceived quantity and is what the research question literally asks about. Includes LLM API latency, all inter-call waits, BoT's retrieval + template instantiation, PAL's Python sandbox execution, and any caching/lookup overhead.\n2. **Secondary attribution \u2014 decomposed components**: LLM-only latency (sum of model call latencies), retrieval/instantiation time (BoT only), Python-exec time (PAL only), number of LLM calls, total input + output tokens. These attribute *where* the extra time goes.\n3. **Secondary proxy \u2014 token count**. Reported because it is hardware-independent and often reported alongside latency, but NOT used to answer the primary question.\n\nReported as latency ratio (e.g., \"BoT is X\u00d7 slower than Expert, 95% CI [L, U]\") on the natural scale; statistical tests run on log-latency because latency is heavy-tailed.\n\n**Per-benchmark sampling strategy.**\n- Use the standard published test instance set for each benchmark (exact instance sets are a researcher decision; not pinned by supplied docs).\n- Each instance runs under each method (within-subjects over methods; this is the right design because it cancels instance difficulty).\n- Each (instance, method) cell runs **k \u2265 3 trials** with different random seeds to capture decoding stochasticity.\n- Randomize the *order* in which methods are applied per instance to prevent systematic order effects (e.g., warm-up effects on a server).\n- Report accuracy as a secondary descriptive metric to flag any speed-vs-accuracy confound (a method that's slower but also more accurate is harder to interpret).\n\n**Analysis plan.**\n- **Primary model**: linear mixed-effects on `log(end-to-end latency)` with fixed effects `method \u00d7 benchmark`, random intercepts for `instance` and `trial-within-instance` (or nested random effects `1|instance` and `1|instance:trial`).\n- **Pairwise contrasts** of interest: BoT vs Expert, BoT vs PAL, PAL vs Expert (and CoT vs all three if CoT is included), within each benchmark. Holm-Bonferroni correction across the contrast family.\n- **Effect sizes**: standardized mean difference on the log scale, back-transformed to a latency ratio (\"BoT takes X times longer\"). Bootstrap 95% CIs (cluster on instance).\n- **Confounders to control**:\n  - Backbone model (fixed across cells \u2192 not a confounder in the mixed model, but must be reported).\n  - Prompt length: include `log(prompt token count)` as a fixed-effect covariate; the marginal effect of `method` then reflects the structural cost beyond prompt size.\n  - PAL Python-exec overhead: include as a covariate in a secondary decomposition model so the LLM-only comparison is apples-to-apples.\n  - BoT retrieval/template overhead: same treatment.\n  - API/server load: random intercept for run-day or run-hour block; alternatively, fully randomized trial ordering.\n- **Pre-registered predictions** (to avoid p-hacking on the headline comparison): \"BoT > PAL > Expert on latency in all three benchmarks, with the largest gap on Checkmate-in-One.\" Deviations are reported as such.\n\n---\n\n## (3) Consequential assumptions and uncertainties to resolve\n\nI am listing these as **decisions you need to make or confirm** before the full design, separate from the fixed task requirements (which I'm taking as: design only, no execution claimed).\n\n1. **Definition of \"inference time\"** \u2014 Default proposal: end-to-end wall-clock per instance is primary; decomposed-component latencies (LLM-only, retrieval, Python-exec) are secondary. Alternatives: pure API latency, token count, or number of model calls. *You pick the primary.*\n\n2. **Backbone LLM** \u2014 BoT, Expert, and PAL were originally reported with different models. A single backbone is the key control; the choice has large cost and latency implications. Need: model name + provider + version pin (e.g., gpt-4o-2024-08-06, or a specific open-weight snapshot). Open-weight vs API has different cost/quota trade-offs.\n\n3. **Decoding settings held fixed** \u2014 Temperature, top-p, max_tokens, stop sequences. Must be identical across method \u00d7 benchmark cells so latency differences are attributable to the method, not sampling variance. Recommend: temperature 0.0 for greedy decoding (then a separate stochastic pilot), or a fixed temperature with k trials.\n\n4. **PAL Python-execution attribution** \u2014 Confidence is highest when end-to-end includes the sandbox time AND a separate LLM-only decomposition is reported. Decision needed: which is primary.\n\n5. **BoT retrieval and template-instantiation attribution** \u2014 Same treatment. The Buffer is pre-built once; the per-instance cost is retrieval + adaptation. Need to decide whether the one-time buffer build is amortized (charge 1/N to each instance) or excluded from per-instance latency.\n\n6. **CoT baseline** \u2014 Not named in your question, but the natural single-query reference point. Recommend including; flag as a decision.\n\n7. **Number of trials per cell** \u2014 k \u2265 3 for variance estimation; more (k = 5 or 10) if pilot variance is high. Bounded by budget.\n\n8. **Sample size per benchmark** \u2014 Game of 24 typically \u2264 ~256 instances; MGSM 250 per language; Checkmate-in-One ~1,257. Decision needed: full sets or stratified subsamples? Pilot data ideally informs; without it, propose power calculation based on assumed effect size and variance.\n\n9. **Accuracy as secondary descriptive** \u2014 Recommend yes, because a latency comparison that ignores accuracy is incomplete (slower but more accurate is a different story).\n\n10. **Environmental / quota constraints** \u2014 `/workspace` environment has network disabled and no GPU; only system Python without preinstalled deps. Execution is therefore impossible here regardless of design. The full design must budget against an external compute/API quota that you specify (model API $/hour limit, wall-clock budget, number of LLM calls allowed).\n\n11. **Hardware variance** \u2014 If running on shared infrastructure, latency has run-to-run noise from server load. Need a blocking/randomization strategy.\n\n12. **Prompt template source** \u2014 We have no supplied prompt templates. We will need to either use published templates from the original papers (researcher decision; not verified here) or co-design them to match in token count where possible.\n\n13. **Statistical unit** \u2014 Per-instance latency is the observation; instance is the random-effect grouping for comparisons across methods. Clarify you agree.\n\nDecisions 1, 2, and 10 are the ones most likely to change the design materially. The remaining are tunable knobs.\n\n---\n\nAwaiting your approval (and answers to the assumptions above, or direction to make defaults explicit) before producing the full design.", "evidence": "Repository inspection of /workspace yielded exactly one file, /workspace/task.json (518 bytes), whose verbatim contents I quoted above. No track.md file exists on disk (find / -maxdepth 4 -name track.md produced no matches). No source documents, code, or benchmark data for BoT, Expert, PAL, Game of 24, MGSM, or Checkmate-in-One are present in the workspace. Broader searches for .md/.txt/.py/.json/.yaml under /workspace returned only task.json. The design sketch therefore parameterizes rather than references any unverified specifics about the named frameworks or benchmarks.", "assumptions": ["Section (3) items 1\u201313 in the answer, especially: (1) inference-time = end-to-end wall-clock primary, (2) backbone LLM TBD by researcher, (3) fixed decoding settings across cells, (4) PAL Python-exec included in end-to-end and decomposed separately, (5) BoT retrieval/instantiation included in end-to-end, one-time buffer build amortized, (6) CoT baseline optional, (7) k\u22653 trials per cell, (8) sample sizes TBD, (9) accuracy reported as secondary descriptive only, (10) external compute/API budget TBD, (11) randomization over run blocks controls server-load variance, (12) prompt templates sourced from original papers (researcher-confirmed), (13) per-instance latency is the statistical unit with instance as the random-effect grouping."], "uncertainties": ["The task brief reiterates that no experiment will be run and no outcome is to be invented; I have respected this and produced a design-only plan.", "Primary definition of inference time is a value judgment that materially changes the answer framing \u2014 see point 1 in \u00a73.", "Backbone LLM and compute/API budget (points 2 and 10) can change sample size, number of trials, and whether decomposition into LLM-only vs. overhead time is feasible.", "Whether to include a CoT baseline is a researcher call; its omission changes the comparison set vs. the question's literal wording.", "The \"increased inference time\" claim is a directional hypothesis; whether the design supports equivalence testing (i.e., can rule out trivial slowdown) vs. superiority testing depends on k and sample size chosen in points 7\u20138.", "The 'track.md' entry inside task.json is a one-line embedding of the design-only constraint; nothing else is supplied under that name."]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-f2643c0b-ada6-4415-be61-246cab25f192", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 12}
