el with a specific version identifier across all method × benchmark cells. Exact model and provider left as a parameter the researcher fills in (e.g., latest pinned snapshot of a strong open or closed model); the design includes a placeholder and explains how the choice affects cost and latency interpretation. (3) Decoding — two-pass design: (a) greedy decoding (temperature = 0.0), k = 1 trial, used for the headline comparison; (b) stochastic decoding (temperature = 0.7, top_p = 1.0), k = 5 trials, used for variance estimation. max_tokens is identical across all cells. (4) PAL Python execution — included in end-to-end; reported as its own decomposed component. Sandbox type and per-session startup overhead recorded as parameters in Section 0. (5) BoT retrieval and template instantiation — included in end-to-end; reported as its own decomposed component. The one-time buffer construction cost is amortized: charge 1/N to each instance that draws from the buffer, where N is the number of exercising instances; document the rule. (6) CoT baseline — include CoT (zero-shot, single-query) as a 4th level; clearly note in the design that this is a researcher extension beyond the literal wording. (7) Trials per cell — k = 1 (greedy) + k = 5 (stochastic) per (instance, method) cell = 6 observations per instance per method, 24 per instance total across 4 methods. (8) Sample sizes — default to the standard published test sets within budget: Game of 24 (commonly 100–256 instances — flag that the exact number must be a researcher-supplied parameter because no source documents exist in this workspace); MGSM (full 250-instances-per-language test set; select 1 canonical language in addition to English, e.g., the most-reported non-English language, as a researcher default); Checkmate-in-One (full 1,257 instances if budget allows, otherwise a stratified random subsample of ≥300 with a documented power calculation). Use within-subjects over methods so each instance is exercised under every