r end-to-end latency than each single-query method on every benchmark, with the largest margin expected on Checkmate-in-One; PAL is expected to add Python-exec overhead above Expert and CoT but below BoT. State this as a hypothesis, not as a finding. Document structure for the full design — produce a single Markdown document with: Section 0 researcher-decided defaults table (one row per item 1–13, with rationale, parameter placeholder if any, and fallback rule); Section 1 hypotheses (the directional predictions above, framed as researcher-stated rather than empirical); Section 2 methods (BoT, Expert, PAL, plus CoT) and their operational definitions in terms of the prompt template + retrieval + execution stack, parameterizing any specifics absent from this workspace; Section 3 benchmarks (Game of 24, MGSM, Checkmate-in-One) and per-benchmark instance sets and stratified subsample rules; Section 4 latency operationalization (primary end-to-end wall-clock, secondary decomposition, tertiary token count); Section 5 sampling and trials (within-subjects over methods, k schedule per item 7, randomization per item 11); Section 6 design controls (fixed backbone and version, fixed decoding settings outside the greedy/stochastic split, prompt-length covariate, server-load blocking, execution environment compatibility); Section 7 analysis plan (linear mixed-effects model per item 13, contrast family, Holm–Bonferroni correction, effect-size reporting with cluster-bootstrap CIs, decomposition models that re-run the main model on LLM-only latency to attribute where the time goes, accuracy reported per (method, benchmark) as descriptive); Section 8 feasibility and budget (call-count estimate, cost ceiling, degradation ladder, explicit statement that the current bubblewrap environment is execution-incapable and that the design is scoped for an external execution environment under the researcher-supplied budget); Section 9 threats to validity (prompt-template variance across papers, backbone-model confound if a single model is not feasible, server-load variance, sample-size limits on Checkmate-in-One if subsampled, absence of source documents in this workspace — design rests on researcher-decided defaults rather than on retrieved source specifics, possible interaction between BoT template-match miss-rate and latency); Section 10 decision log mapping each item 1–13 to the resolution above and noting where the final design remains agnostic to a specific value the researcher must supply. Output format: one self-contained Markdown document; no code listings, no execution logs, no fabricated latency numbers. Reference the supplied study context items (Expert Prompting, PAL, BoT, single-query baselines, BoT template-reuse reasoning) as the fixed comparator set; do not introduce additional method levels beyond the four specified (BoT, Expert, PAL, CoT). Proceed to produce the full design.", "required_handoff_stage": "result", "approach_approved": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 14}
