(c) Python-exec time (PAL-only), (d) number of LLM calls. Tertiary proxy: total input + output tokens. | Primary metric matches the user-perceived quantity the question literally asks about. Decomposition attributes where time goes; token count alone is explicitly insufficient. | Hardware-clock library or virtual clock at process boundary; per-call latency JSON from API. | If end-to-end cannot be measured cleanly (e.g., shared host), fall back to (process-start to process-end) wall-clock with documented exclusion of any unavoidable I/O. |\n| 2 | Backbone LLM | A single frontier model pinned to a specific version identifier across all method × benchmark × trial cells. | The model is the central confound for latency (and cost). Holding it fixed is the only way to attribute latency differences to the method. | `BACKBONE_MODEL` = researcher-supplied (e.g., a specific pinned snapshot of a strong closed or open-weight model), `BACKBONE_PROVIDER`, `BACKBONE_VERSION`. | If a single backbone across all four methods is infeasible, document per-method substitutions and report per-method model as a covariate; downgrade to \"exploratory\" status. |\n| 3 | Decoding settings | Two-pass: (a) greedy (temperature = 0.0, k = 1) for the headline comparison; (b) stochastic (temperature = 0.7, top_p = 1.0, k = 5) for variance estimation. `max_tokens` identical across all cells. | Greedy removes decoding variance from the headline latency comparison; stochastic pass estimates run-to-run variance and supports CIs. | `MAX_TOKENS` = single value chosen to exceed the longest expected generation across all methods. | If `max_tokens` differs by method in a way that cannot be normalized, drop PAL or BoT longest-tail cases; report censored-token counts. |\n| 4 | PAL Python execution attribution | Included in end-to-end primary; reported as the (c) secondary component. | The user pays for sandbox time; excluding it would overstate PAL's advantage. Separating it lets us attribute the gap. | `PYTHON_SANDBOX` = researcher-supplied (e.g., local subprocess, container, or restricted-interpreter stub); `SANDBOX_STARTUP_S` = measured per session; method-specific warm-up excluded from per-instance latency if amortizable. | If sandbox startup is non-trivial and per-session, amortize across session instances and document the rule. |\n| 5 | BoT retrieval and template instantiation attribution | Included in end-to-end primary; reported as the (b) secondary component. One-time buffer-construction cost amortized at 1/N per exercising instance, where N = number of instances in the benchmark that draw from the buffer. | Matches what each instance actually pays. Amortization prevents giving BoT an unfair fixed-charge penalty or unfair fixed-charge credit. | `BUFFER_BUILD_S` = researcher-measured; `N_EXERCISING` = per-benchmark count. | If the buffer is rebuilt per method/dataset, document the cost and treat the rebuild as part of the secondary decomposition only. |\n| 6 | CoT baseline | Add Chain-of-Thought (zero-shot, single-query) as a 4th method level. Document as researcher extension beyond the literal wording of the question. | CoT is the conventional single-query reference; without it, the \"single-query\" comparator set is under-specified. | `COT_PROMPT_TEMPLATE` = researcher-supplied (zero-shot phrasing, no exemplars). | If the researcher prefers strictly the literal three methods, drop CoT and document the deviation from this default. |\n| 7 | Trials per cell | Greedy k = 1 + stochastic k = 5 → 6 observations per (instance, method); 24 per instance across the 4 methods. | Anchors the headline point estimate on greedy (deterministic) and keeps variance estimation feasible. | `K_GREEDY = 1`, `K_STOCHASTIC = 5`. | Degradation ladder (item 10). |\n| 8 | Sample sizes | Within-subjects over methods. Game of 24: full standard test set (commonly 100–256 — exact value is a researcher parameter). MGSM: full 250-instances-per-language test set, with researcher default of English plus one canonical non-English language (researcher selects). Checkmate-in-One: full 1,257 instances if budget allows, otherwise a stratified random subsample of ≥ 300. | Within-subjects cancels instance-difficulty variance, which is large. Stratified subsampling preserves benchmark coverage. | `N_G24`, `N_MGSM_PER_LANG`, `N_CHECKMATE`, `MGSM_LANGS` (researcher-supplied; default: English + 1 canonical non-English). | If budget is insufficient, fall through the ladder in item 10. |\n| 9 | Accuracy | Reported per (method, benchmark) as a secondary descriptive metric alongside latency; not used to gate the latency comparison. | A method that is slower but more accurate is a different finding from one that is slower and equally accurate. Accuracy gives the reader the means to interpret the latency gap without conflating the two. | Accuracy judged against each benchmark's standard reference (e.g., puzzle solved = all four numbers combined to 24; MGSM answer equals ground truth within numeric tolerance; Checkmate move is legal and mates in one against the stated position). | If accuracy is the published metric for a benchmark, use that; if not, define cell-level correctness per the benchmark convention. |\n| 10 | Compute / API budget | Researcher-supplied ceiling: total API spend ($), wall-clock duration (h), max model calls. Up-front call-count estimate = Σ_instances × 4 methods × 6 trials (i.e., 24 × Σ_instances). Degradation ladder on budget pressure: (i) reduce stochastic k from 5 to 3, saving 2 stochastic trials per cell; (ii) reduce instances via stratified subsample (item 8 fallback); (iii) drop the stochastic pass and report only greedy; (iv) drop CoT last. | The call-count estimate determines feasibility and where the experiment lands on the ladder. The ladder is documented in advance to avoid ad-hoc cuts after seeing data. | `BUDGET_USD`, `BUDGET_HOURS`, `BUDGET_MAX_CALLS` (all researcher-supplied). | Ladder order is fixed; the rung hit is reported alongside results. |\n| 11 | Run-block randomization | Fully randomize trial order over (method × trial × instance), stratified across 4 × 6 h time blocks (24 h). Server-load variance is then modeled as a random effect or absorbed by blocking. | Randomization + blocking is the standard defense against systematic latency drift over wall-clock time (warm hardware, diurnal load, rate-limit tail effects). | `N_BLOCKS = 4`, `BLOCK_HOURS = 6` (parameters). | If the experiment must run longer than 24 h, increase block count and document. |\n| 12 | Prompt templates | Sourced from the original BoT, Expert, and PAL papers as documented in those papers (researcher-supplied because no workspace documents are available). Where feasible, approximate prompt length matched across methods. `log(prompt_tokens)` included as a fixed-effect covariate so residual prompt-length differences are not attributed to the method effect. | Different prompt templates have different token counts; left unadjusted, a longer prompt is a hidden latency tax. Covariating out prompt length leaves the structural method effect. | Per-method prompt template strings plus measured token counts. | If a paper's templates are unavailable, document the replacement template as a deviation. |\n| 13 | Statistical unit and model | Observation = per-instance per-trial latency. Primary model: `log(end-to-end latency) ~ method × benchmark + log(prompt_tokens) + (1 | instance) + (1 | instance:trial) + (1 | run_block)`. Random-effects grouping for across-method comparisons = instance. Standardized mean differences on the log scale, back-transformed to latency ratios. 95% CIs from cluster-bootstrap on instance. | Log-transform stabilizes heavy-tailed latency variance. Mixed-effects handles the within-instance correlation across methods and trials and blocks on server load. | If a mixed-effects package is unavailable in the execution environment, fall back to a fixed-effects regression with cluster-robust SEs at the instance level; document the substitution. |\n\n---\n\n## Section 1 — Pre-registered directional hypotheses\n\nThe following are **researcher-stated predictions, not empirical results.** They are declared in advance to discipline the analysis and to make deviations reportable as such.\n\n- **H1 (primary).** BoT incurs higher mean end-to-end wall-clock latency per instance than each single-query method (Expert, PAL, CoT) on every benchmark.\n- **H2 (secondary).** The latency gap between BoT and the single-query methods is **largest on Checkmate-in-One** and **smallest on Game of 24** (consistent with BoT's template-reuse mechanism being most valuable when retrieved templates cover many instances, but costing more per instance than direct single-query calls).\n- **H3 (secondary).** PAL's mean end-to-end latency falls above Expert and CoT (due to Python-exec overhead) but below BoT.\n- **H4 (decomposition).** The bulk of the BoT vs. Expert/CoT latency gap is attributable to the secondary component (b) BoT retrieval/instantiation plus additional LLM calls, not to the LLM call being slower per call. The PAL vs. Expert/CoT gap is attributable to component (c) Python execution.\n- **H5 (descriptive).** Accuracy differences, if any, are within ±10 percentage points across methods on each benchmark. The headline conclusion should not depend on accuracy being identical; if it is not, accuracy is reported alongside each latency number.\n\n**Contrast family** (within each benchmark): BoT vs Expert, BoT vs PAL, BoT vs CoT, PAL vs Expert, PAL vs CoT, Expert vs CoT. Correction: Holm–Bonferroni across the family.\n\n---\n\n## Section 2 — Methods (operational definitions)\n\nThe workspace contains no source papers or code for any of the four methods. Each method is therefore specified as a **design-level operationalization**, parameterized where specifics are absent. Times are measured around the operational steps described below.\n\n### 2.1 CoT (zero-shot, single-query) — researcher-added reference level\n\n- **Inputs:** benchmark instance text.\n- **Steps:** (1) construct prompt from `COT_PROMPT_TEMPLATE` (zero-shot, no exemplars, encouraging step-by-step reasoning); (2) issue exactly **one** LLM call; (3) parse the model's free-form answer.\n- **Time components contributing to end-to-end:** LLM call latency only. No retrieval, no execution.\n- **Researcher-supplied:** `COT_PROMPT_TEMPLATE` (string), `ANSWER_PARSER` (regex or LLM-assisted extraction).\n\n### 2.2 Expert Prompting (single-query, expert persona)\n\n- **Inputs:** benchmark instance text + expert persona specification.\n- **Steps:** (1) construct prompt from `EXPERT_PROMPT_TEMPLATE` (an expert-persona-prefixed single-shot prompt); (2) issue exactly **one** LLM call; (3) parse the model's free-form answer.\n- **Time components:** LLM call latency only.\n- **Researcher-supplied:** `EXPERT_PROMPT_TEMPLATE` (string), persona specification (domain, role, expertise level).\n\n### 2.3 PAL (Program-Aided Language) — single-query with code execution\n\n- **Inputs:** benchmark instance text.\n- **Steps:** (1) construct prompt from `PAL_PROMPT_TEMPLATE` (elicits Python code solving the instance); (2) issue exactly **one** LLM call → response contains code; (3) parse the code block; (4) execute code in `PYTHON_SANDBOX` (with timeout `PAL_EXEC_TIMEOUT_S`); (5) from the execution output, derive the final answer; (6) if parsing or execution fails, record a failure rather than retrying (a retry would conflate method reliability with method latency).\n- **Time components:** LLM latency + Python-exec time + parsing/extraction time.\n- **Researcher-supplied:** `PAL_PROMPT_TEMPLATE`, `PYTHON_SANDBOX` (sandbox type), `PAL_EXEC_TIMEOUT_S`, sandbox-startup measurement rule (item 4).\n\n### 2.4 BoT (Buffer of Thoughts) — retrieval + template instantiation\n\n- **Inputs:** benchmark instance text.\n- **Steps:** (1) encode input into a retrieval key per the buffer's retrieval protocol (placeholder — exact key-construction method is researcher-supplied because no workspace documents are available); (2) retrieve top-`k_TEMPLATES` templates from the pre-built buffer; (3) select a template (researcher-supplied selector: e.g., the highest-scoring template, with the scoring rule also researcher-supplied); (4) instantiate the selected template with the instance-specific values; (5) optionally make further LLM calls on the instantiated template (the number of follow-up calls is method-structural and reported as the (d) secondary component); (6) extract the final answer.\n- **Time components:** LLM-only latency **+** BoT retrieval/instantiation time **+** parsing/extraction time.\n- **Buffer construction:** done **once** per benchmark, off the measurement path for instance latency, with cost amortized at 1/N (item 5).\n- **Researcher-supplied:** retrieval protocol, `k_TEMPLATES`, template selector, instantiation procedure, follow-up call structure, buffer contents (templates keyed by problem class). Because the workspace has no BoT source documents, all of the above are researcher-supplied and listed in Section 10 as items remaining agnostic to a specific value.\n\n### 2.5 Operational stack shared across all methods\n\n- **Prompt assembly:** pure string concatenation, measured separately and added to component (b) for BoT retrieval/instantiation only (since it is method-structural there); for the single-query methods, prompt assembly is part of LLM-call setup and folded into (a).\n- **LLM call envelope:** identical HTTP/gRPC client options across methods (retry policy, timeout, connection pooling). Any retry would be a confound and is therefore disabled.\n- **Answer extraction:** measured as part of the method's own component (b)/(c); not separately reported unless it dominates.\n\n---\n\n## Section 3 — Benchmarks\n\nEach benchmark's instance set is parameterized because no source files are present in the workspace. The within-subjects design (Section 5) requires every instance to be run under every method.\n\n### 3.1 Game of 24\n\n- **Task:** given four integers, combine with `+ − × ÷` and parentheses to reach 24.\n- **Standard test set size:** commonly 100–256 published instances (researcher-supplied exact `N_G24`).\n- **Correctness:** success iff the model's (or, for PAL, the executed program's) outcome is exactly 24 and the expression uses each input exactly once.\n- **Instance structure:** LLM call typically trivial in token count; PAL execution and BoT instantiation are the dominant non-LLM time costs.\n\n### 3.2 MGSM (Multilingual Grade-School Math)\n\n- **Task:** word problem, multilingual.\n- **Standard test set:** 250 instances per language, multiple languages available.\n- **Researcher default language selection:** English plus one canonical non-English language. The non-English language is itself a researcher default parameter (`MGSM_LANGS`); the default is \"the most-reported non-English language in published MGSM work\" with the precise language a researcher-supplied parameter.\n- **Correctness:** numeric final answer matches ground truth within standard tolerance per published MGSM convention.\n- **Instance structure:** token counts vary by language; `log(prompt_tokens)` covariate covers this.\n\n### 3.3 Checkmate-in-One\n\n- **Task:** given a chess position, output a legal move that delivers checkmate on the next move.\n- **Standard test set:** 1,257 instances (researcher-supplied default); stratified random subsample of ≥ 300 instances if budget does not allow the full set.\n- **Stratification rule:** preserve the published difficulty distribution (e.g., bucket by positions-to-checkmate depth or by puzzle-source partition); document the stratification variable.\n- **Correctness:** the predicted move (or, for PAL, the move computed by the executed code) is legal in the given position and mates in one against a responding engine.\n- **Instance structure:** typically expressed in FEN or SAN; encoding/decoding is method-invariant and not included in latency.\n\n### 3.4 Instance-set parameter summary\n\n| Benchmark | Researcher parameter | Default if unspecified |\n|-----------|----------------------|------------------------|\n| Game of 24 | `N_G24`, instance file | Full standard test set (commonly 100–256) |\n| MGSM | `MGSM_LANGS`, instance file | English + 1 canonical non-English, full 250/lang |\n| Checkmate-in-One | `N_CHECKMATE`, instance file | Full 1,257, stratified random subsample ≥ 300 if needed |\n\n---\n\n## Section 4 — Latency operationalization\n\n### 4.1 Primary metric\n\n`LAT_PRIMARY[i, m, t]` = end-to-end wall-clock seconds, measured from `t_system_receives_input[i]` to `t_system_returns_final_answer[i, m, t]`, where `i` indexes instance, `m ∈ {CoT, Expert, PAL, BoT}`, and `t` indexes trial. A single stopwatch-style record is taken at process boundary; sub-millisecond resolution is unnecessary because API latency dominates.\n\n### 4.2 Secondary decomposition\n\nRecorded as six auxiliary columns per observation:\n\n1. `LAT_LLM[i, m, t]` — sum of LLM-call latencies across all calls in trial. For CoT/Expert this is `LAT_PRIMARY`; for PAL the LLM-only portion; for BoT the LLM-only portion across all calls.\n2. `LAT_RETRIEVE[i, m, t]` — time spent on BoT retrieval + template instantiation (zero for non-BoT methods).\n3. `LAT_PYEXEC[i, m, t]` — Python sandbox execution time (zero for non-PAL methods).\n4. `N_LLM_CALLS[i, m, t]` — integer count of LLM calls.\n5. `LAT_PARSE[i, m, t]` — parsing/extraction time (zero for CoT/Expert).\n6. `OVERHEAD[i, m, t]` = `LAT_PRIMARY − (LAT_LLM + LAT_RETRIEVE + LAT_PYEXEC + LAT_PARSE)` — residual overhead from inter-call waits, network, scheduling.\n\nThese six columns support the H4 attribution: where the BoT/predicted gap actually lives.\n\n### 4.3 Tertiary proxy\n\n`TOK_TOTAL[i, m, t]` = `input_tokens` + `output_tokens` summed across all LLM calls in the trial. Explicitly **not** the answer to the research question; reported to support comparability with prior work that reports tokens instead of seconds. The design carries a stand-alone caveat in Section 9 (Threats to validity): latency conclusions cannot be drawn from `TOK_TOTAL` alone because tokens do not capture Python-execution time or sandbox-startup overhead.\n\n### 4.4 What is *not* measured\n\n- One-time buffer construction for BoT (excluded from per-instance latency, amortized as 1/N into a single reporting line per benchmark).\n- Benchmark data loading from disk (excluded by starting the clock only after the input is in memory).\n- Result logging and upload (excluded by stopping the clock before the answer is handed to the logger).\n\n---\n\n## Section 5 — Sampling and trials\n\n### 5.1 Within-subjects design\n\nEvery instance `i` is exercised under every method `m`. This cancels instance-difficulty variance from the method comparison and is the single most important structural choice in the design. Implementation: a single random permutation over (instance, method, trial) tuples per run block.\n\n### 5.2 Trial schedule (per item 7)\n\n- Greedy pass: k = 1, temperature = 0.0, deterministic.\n- Stochastic pass: k = 5, temperature = 0.7, top_p = 1.0.\n- Total per (instance, method): 6 trials = 24 trials per instance across the 4 methods.\n\n### 5.3 Greedy vs. stochastic split\n\nThe greedy k = 1 observation is the **headline** per-cell latency number (no decoding variance). The stochastic k = 5 observations are pooled (mean, SD) to estimate run-to-run variance; the headline report card uses the greedy point estimate and a ±1 SE band derived from the stochastic SD.\n\n### 5.4 Run-block randomization (per item 11)\n\n- The 4 × 6 = 24 h time block scheme applies to the actual execution environment, not to the workspace.\n- Within each block, trial order is a uniform random permutation of all (instance × method × trial) cells, with the constraint that the same instance is not adjacent in the permutation (to spread warm-up effects across methods).\n- A trial-level `RUN_BLOCK` column is recorded for the mixed-effects model.\n\n### 5.5 Failure handling\n\nIf PAL execution times out or raises an exception, the trial is recorded as `LAT_PRIMARY` including the timeout duration (i.e., the user pays the timeout), `SUCCESS = false`, and the accuracy cell is `failure`. The design does **not** retry on failure because retries would conflate method reliability with method latency. Failures are reported as a separate row aggregate in the analysis.\n\n---\n\n## Section 6 — Controls\n\n| Confound | Control |\n|----------|---------|\n| Backbone model | Single `BACKBONE_MODEL` pinned to `BACKBONE_VERSION`, identical client options, identical `MAX_TOKENS` (item 2). |\n| Decoding stochasticity | Greedy pass for headline; stochastic k = 5 for variance (item 3). |\n| Prompt length | Approximate length matching across methods where feasible; precise adjustment via `log(prompt_tokens)` covariate in the mixed-effects model (item 12). |\n| PAL Python-exec overhead | Measured as `LAT_PYEXEC` and reported separately so the LLM-only comparison is apples-to-apples (items 1, 4). |\n| BoT retrieval/instantiation overhead | Measured as `LAT_RETRIEVE` and reported separately; one-time buffer build amortized 1/N (items 1, 5). |\n| Server-load variance | Randomization + 4 × 6 h blocking + `(1 | run_block)` random effect (item 11). |\n| Retry storm | Retries disabled at the HTTP client; PAL failures recorded but not retried (item 5.5). |\n| Clock drift | Single monotonic clock source (e.g., `time.monotonic_ns()`); reported wall-clock vs. monotonic clock are not aliased. |\n| Order effects | Within-block uniform random permutation of (instance, method, trial) tuples (item 5.4). |\n| Sandbox startup | Per-session startup measured; if significant, amortized across session instances (item 4). |\n\n---\n\n## Section 7 — Analysis plan\n\n### 7.1 Primary regression (per item 13)\n\n```\nlog(LAT_PRIMARY[i, m, t, b]) \n    ~ method × benchmark \n    + log(prompt_tokens[i, m]) \n    + (1 | instance) \n    + (1 | instance:trial) \n    + (1 | run_block)\n```\n\n- `b` indexes run block.\n- Random intercept for `instance` captures instance-difficulty baseline latency; for across-method comparisons, instance is the clustering unit.\n- `(1 | instance:trial)` allows the same instance to have different intercepts across trials (e.g., one stochastic trial happens to hit a cache).\n- `(1 | run_block)` absorbs wall-clock drift in server conditions.\n\n### 7.2 Contrast family (within each benchmark)\n\nBoT vs Expert, BoT vs PAL, BoT vs CoT, PAL vs Expert, PAL vs CoT, Expert vs CoT.\n\nCorrection: Holm–Bonferroni across the family. The pre-registered H1 sets the directional alternative for the BoT-vs-single-query contrasts; the others are two-sided.\n\n### 7.3 Effect-size reporting\n\n- Estimate: coefficient on `method` (on log scale), back-transformed to a latency ratio (\"BoT takes β× longer than the reference method\").\n- 95% CI: cluster-bootstrap on instance, 10,000 resamples, stratified by benchmark.\n- Standardized mean difference (Hedges' g on log-latency) reported for completeness.\n\n### 7.4 Decomposition models\n\nTo attribute where the time goes, re-run the primary regression four times, each time substituting the dependent variable:\n\n- `log(LAT_LLM)` — LLM-only latency (pal vs. CoT/Expert gap should collapse if execute is the cost).\n- `log(LAT_LLM) + LAT_RETRIEVE + LAT_PYEXEC` — clear-the-non-LLM-overhead version.\n- `N_LLM_CALLS` (Poisson or negative-binomial GLMM) — does BoT's gap reduce to a call-count gap?\n- `log(LAT_PRIMARY)` with random effects replaced by instance clustering only as a robustness check.\n\nThese directly test H4.\n\n### 7.5 Accuracy (per item 9)\n\nReported per (method, benchmark) cell as the proportion of trials where `SUCCESS = true`, alongside the latency table. **Not** entered as a covariate into the latency model. The two numbers are reported side by side so the reader can interpret the latency gap (faster-but-worse, slower-but-better, etc.).\n\n### 7.6 Sample exclusion rules (pre-specified)\n\nThe following are the only exclusions permitted, and each is declared in advance:\n\n1. Trials where `BACKBONE_MODEL` returned a non-response or refused (counted, not excluded).\n2. Trials where `LAT_PRIMARY` exceeds `MAX_TRIAL_LATENCY_S` (researcher parameter; defaults to 10× the median for that cell) — flagged, not silently dropped.\n3. Pre-block warm-up trials (if any are run) — excluded only if they are explicitly labeled warm-up.\n\nNo other exclusions. All deviations are documented in the decision log (Section 10).\n\n---\n\n## Section 8 — Feasibility and budget\n\n### 8.1 Up-front call-count estimate\n\nPer item 7: each (instance, method) cell requires 6 LLM calls (1 greedy + 5 stochastic). Total:\n\n```\nTOTAL_CALLS = (N_G24 + N_MGSM_PER_LANG × |MGSM_LANGS| + N_CHECKMATE) × 4 × 6\n```\n\nExample order-of-magnitude under default sizes:\n\n| Benchmark | N | Methods | Trials | Calls |\n|-----------|---|---------|--------|-------|\n| Game of 24 | 256 | 4 | 6 | 6,144 |\n| MGSM (2 langs × 250) | 500 | 4 | 6 | 12,000 |\n| Checkmate-in-One (full) | 1,257 | 4 | 6 | 30,168 |\n| **Total** | **2,013** | **4** | **6** | **48,312** |\n\nPlus buffer construction for BoT (one-off, off the measurement path; researcher-supplied buffer-build iteration count).\n\n### 8.2 Cost projection\n\nPer-call cost depends on `BACKBONE_MODEL`, input/output token counts, and provider pricing. Cost is reported as `TOTAL_CALLS × avg_cost_per_call(i, m, trial)` against `BUDGET_USD`. The design does not invent a per-call dollar figure because no model identity is pinned in this workspace.\n\n### 8.3 Degradation ladder (per item 10)\n\nOn budget pressure, apply in this fixed order:\n\n1. Reduce stochastic `K_STOCHASTIC` from 5 to 3. Saves 2 trials per cell; greedy headline unchanged.\n2. Reduce instances via the per-benchmark fallback in item 8 (MGSM: single-language; Game of 24: shorter published test set variant; Checkmate-in-One: stratified subsample down to ≥ 300).\n3. Drop the stochastic pass; report greedy only. Removes variance estimation; effect sizes lose CIs from cluster bootstrap.\n4. Drop CoT last. Reduces method count from 4 to 3 (BoT, Expert, PAL — the literal question's three).\n\nThe rung actually applied is reported alongside results.\n\n### 8.4 Environment capability statement\n\nThe supplied workspace (`bubblewrap` runtime, network disabled, no GPU, system Python only, no preinstalled dependencies) is **execution-incapable for the proposed experiment.** This document is therefore scoped for **an external execution environment** that the researcher will run against, against the budget ceilings in item 10. The design carries no claim of execution in `/workspace` and no latency numbers derived from such execution.\n\n---\n\n## Section 9 — Threats to validity\n\n1. **Prompt-template variance across methods.** CoT, Expert, PAL, and BoT use structurally different prompts. Even with the `log(prompt_tokens)` covariate, residual prompt-content effects (e.g., exemplars vs. no exemplars, persona vs. plain) may bias the latency comparison. *Mitigation:* per-method published templates used; sensitivity analysis rerun with `log(prompt_tokens)` removed to estimate upper-bound regression-omission bias.\n2. **Backbone-model confound under substitution.** If a single backbone model is refused or unavailable for one method (e.g., a constraint only specific to a provider), per-method backbone substitution re-introduces model confounds. *Mitigation:* researcher locks the binding via item 2 default; substitution is a deviation and is logged.\n3. **Server-load variance.** The 4 × 6 h blocking + random permutation is a partial defense; severe load spikes may survive blocking. *Mitigation:* the `(1 | run_block)` random effect absorbs residual drift; sensitivity analysis rerun without it.\n4. **Sample-size limits on Checkmate-in-One.** If the budget ladder (item 10) reaches rung 2 or 3, the analysis has fewer Checkmate-in-One observations. With stratification, the estimate remains representative of the full set, but the SD inflates. *Mitigation:* power calculation reported; if SD × √(1/n) exceeds the smallest BoT-vs-CoT effect size of interest, the experiment is reported as under-powered for that contrast.\n5. **Absence of source documents in the workspace.** This design rests entirely on researcher-decided defaults (Section 0). All method-specific prompts, retrieval selectors, buffer contents, benchmark instance lists, and answer parsers are researcher-supplied parameters because no source material is present in `/workspace`. *Mitigation:* every researcher-supplied parameter is enumerated in Section 10 and is auditable post-hoc.\n6. **BoT template-match miss-rate interaction with latency.** If the buffer does not contain a template for an instance, BoT either falls back to a default (slow path) or fails; either outcome inflates BoT's measured latency in ways that are method-internal rather than structural. *Mitigation:* miss-rate is reported per benchmark and is treated as a method attribute; the secondary decomposition separates LLM-only latency so the non-LLM cost driver is visible.\n7. **PAL Python-execution timeout conflation.** A timed-out PAL trial costs `PAL_EXEC_TIMEOUT_S` worth of latency; if PAL is more likely to time out on harder instances, the latency estimate is biased upward on those instances. *Mitigation:* timeouts are recorded and reported separately; the headline latency excludes `LAT_PRIMARY` from timed-out trials in a sensitivity analysis.\n8. **MGSM language selection confound.** Choosing one canonical non-English language embeds that language's tokenization cost into the cross-method comparison. *Mitigation:* language enters the mixed model as a fixed effect or the comparator is reported within each language separately.\n9. **Checkmate-in-One answer parsing.** Move parsing from SAN/FEN is non-trivial;