tains a single file, `/workspace/task.json`, which embeds a one-line `documents.track.md` field stating \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\" No source papers, prompt templates, code, benchmark files, or implementation notes for BoT, Expert Prompting, PAL, Game of 24, MGSM, or Checkmate-in-One are present in `/workspace`. Wherever the design needs a method-specific or benchmark-specific value that is not supplied in the workspace, that value is treated as a **researcher-supplied parameter** and explicitly parameterized rather than fabricated.\n\n---\n\n## Section 0 \u2014 Researcher-decided defaults\n\n| # | Decision | Default / parameter | Rationale | Parameter placeholder(s) | Fallback rule |\n|---|----------|---------------------|-----------|--------------------------|---------------|\n| 1 | Definition of inference time | Primary: end-to-end wall-clock per instance (system-receives-input \u2192 system-returns-final-answer), including all LLM API latency, inter-call waits, BoT retrieval, BoT template instantiation, and PAL Python execution. Secondary (decomposed): (a) LLM-only latency = sum of model-call latencies, (b) retrieval + template-instantiation time (BoT-only), (c) Python-exec time (PAL-only), (d) number of LLM calls. Tertiary proxy: total input + output tokens. | Primary metric matches the user-perceived quantity the question literally asks about. Decomposition attributes where time goes; token count alone is explicitly insufficient. | Hardware-clock library or virtual clock at process boundary; per-call latency JSON from API. | If end-to-end cannot be measured cleanly (e.g., shared host), fall back to (process-start to process-end) wall-clock with documented exclusion of any unavoidable I/O. |\n| 2 | Backbone LLM | A single frontier model pinned to a specific version identifier across all method \u00d7 benchmark \u00d7 trial cells. | The model is the central confound for latency (and cost). Holding it fixed is the only way to attribute latency differences to the method. | `BACKBONE_MODEL`, `BACKBONE_PROVIDER`, `BACKBONE_VERSION` \u2014 all researcher-supplied. | If a single backbone across all four methods is infeasible, document per-method substitutions and report per-method model as a covariate; downgrade to \"exploratory\" status. |\n| 3 | Decoding settings | Two-pass: (a) greedy (temperature = 0.0, k = 1) for the headline comparison; (b) stochastic (temperature = 0.7, top_p = 1.0, k = 5) for variance estimation. `max_tokens` identical across all cells. | Greedy removes decoding variance from the headline latency comparison; stochastic pass estimates run-to-run variance and supports CIs. | `MAX_TOKENS` \u2014 single value chosen to exceed the longest expected generation across all methods. | If `max_tokens` differs by method in a way that cannot be normalized, drop PAL or BoT longest-tail cases; report censored-token counts. |\n| 4 | PAL Python execution attribution | Included in end-to-end primary; reported as the (c) secondary component. | The user pays for sandbox time; excluding it would overstate PAL's advantage. Separating it lets us attribute the gap. | `PYTHON_SANDBOX`, `SANDBOX_STARTUP_S`, `PAL_EXEC_TIMEOUT_S` \u2014 researcher-supplied. | If sandbox startup is non-trivial and per-session, amortize across session instances and document the rule. |\n| 5 | BoT retrieval and template instantiation attribution | Included in end-to-end primary; reported as the (b) secondary component. One-time buffer-construction cost amortized at 1/N per exercising instance, where N = number of instances in the benchmark that draw from the buffer. | Matches what each instance actually pays. Amortization prevents giving BoT an unfair fixed-charge penalty or unfair fixed-charge credit. | `BUFFER_BUILD_S`, `N_EXERCISING` per benchmark. | If the buffer is rebuilt per method/dataset, document the cost and treat the rebuild as part of the secondary decomposition only. |\n| 6 | CoT baseline | Add Chain-of-Thought (zero-shot, single-query) as a 4th method level. Document as researcher extension beyond the literal wording of the question. | CoT is the conventional single-query reference; without it, the \"single-query\" comparator set is under-specified. | `COT_PROMPT_TEMPLATE` \u2014 researcher-supplied zero-shot phrasing. | If the researcher prefers strictly the literal three methods, drop CoT and document the deviation from this default. |\n| 7 | Trials per cell | Greedy k = 1 + stochastic k = 5 \u2192 6 observations per (instance, method); 24 per instance across the 4 methods. | Anchors the headline point estimate on greedy (deterministic) and keeps variance estimation feasible. | `K_GREEDY = 1`, `K_STOCHASTIC = 5`. | Degradation ladder (item 10). |\n| 8 | Sample sizes | Within-subjects over methods. Game of 24: full standard test set (commonly 100\u2013256 \u2014 exact value is a researcher parameter). MGSM: full 250-instances-per-language test set, with researcher default of English plus one canonical non-English language. Checkmate-in-One: full 1,257 instances if budget allows, otherwise a stratified random subsample of \u2265 300. | Within-subjects cancels instance-difficulty variance. Stratified subsampling preserves benchmark coverage. | `N_G24`, `N_MGSM_PER_LANG`, `N_CHECKMATE`, `MGSM_LANGS` \u2014 researcher-supplied. | If budget is insufficient, fall through the ladder in item 10. |\n| 9 | Accuracy | Reported per (method, benchmark) as a secondary descriptive metric alongside latency; not used to gate the latency comparison. | A method that is slower but more accurate is a different finding. Accuracy gives the reader the means to interpret the latency gap without conflating the two. | Per-benchmark correctness rule (e.g., puzzle solved, numeric tolerance, engine-validated mate). | If accuracy is the published metric for a benchmark, use that; if not, define cell-level correctness per the benchmark convention. |\n| 10 | Compute / API budget | Researcher-supplied ceiling: total API spend ($), wall-clock duration (h), max model calls. Call-count estimate = `\u03a3_instances \u00d7 4 \u00d7 6`. Degradation ladder on budget pressure: (i) reduce stochastic k from 5 to 3; (ii) reduce instances via stratified subsample; (iii) drop stochastic pass; (iv) drop CoT last. | The ladder is documented in advance to avoid ad-hoc cuts after seeing data. | `BUDGET_USD`, `BUDGET_HOURS`, `BUDGET_MAX_CALLS`. | Ladder order is fixed; the rung hit is reported alongside results. |\n| 11 | Run-block randomization | Fully randomize over (method \u00d7 trial \u00d7 instance), stratified across 4 \u00d7 6 h time blocks (24 h total). Server-load variance is modeled as a random effect or absorbed by blocking. | Randomization + blocking is the standard defense against systematic latency drift over wall-clock time. | `N_BLOCKS = 4`, `BLOCK_HOURS = 6`. | If the experiment runs longer than 24 h, increase block count and document. |\n| 12 | Prompt templates | Sourced from the original BoT, Expert, and PAL papers as documented in those papers (researcher-supplied because no workspace documents are available). Approximate prompt length matched across methods where feasible. `log(prompt_tokens)` as a fixed-effect covariate. | Different prompts have different token counts; without adjustment, longer prompts are a hidden latency tax. | Per-method prompt template strings and measured token counts. | If a paper's templates are unavailable, document the replacement template as a deviation. |\n| 13 | Statistical unit and model | Observation = per-instance per-trial latency. Primary model: `log(end-to-end latency) ~ method \u00d7 benchmark + log(prompt_tokens) + (1|instance) + (1|instance:trial) + (1|run_block)`. Random-effects grouping = instance. Standardized mean differences on the log scale, back-transformed to latency ratios; 95% CIs from cluster-bootstrap on instance. | Log-transform stabilizes heavy-tailed latency variance. Mixed-effects handles within-instance correlation. | None \u2014 fully specified. | Fixed-effects regression with cluster-robust SEs at the instance level if a mixed-effects package is unavailable. |\n\n---\n\n## Section 1 \u2014 Pre-registered directional hypotheses\n\nThe following are **researcher-stated predictions, not empirical results.**\n\n- **H1 (primary).** BoT incurs higher mean end-to-end wall-clock latency per instance than each single-query method (Expert, PAL, CoT) on every benchmark.\n- **H2 (secondary).** The latency gap between BoT and the single-query methods is **largest on Checkmate-in-One** and **smallest on Game of 24**.\n- **H3 (secondary).** PAL's mean end-to-end latency falls above Expert and CoT (due to Python-exec overhead) but below BoT.\n- **H4 (decomposition).** The BoT-vs-Expert/CoT gap is attributable primarily to the secondary component (b) BoT retrieval/instantiation plus additional LLM calls. The PAL-vs-Expert/CoT gap is attributable to component (c) Python execution.\n- **H5 (descriptive).** Accuracy differences, if any, are within \u00b110 percentage points across methods on each benchmark.\n\n**Contrast family** (within each benchmark): BoT vs Expert, BoT vs PAL, BoT vs CoT, PAL vs Expert, PAL vs CoT, Expert vs CoT. Correction: Holm\u2013Bonferroni across the family.\n\n---\n\n## Section 2 \u2014 Methods (operational definitions)\n\nThe workspace contains no source papers or code for any of the four methods. Each method is therefore specified as a **design-level operationalization**, parameterized where specifics are absent.\n\n### 2.1 CoT (zero-shot, single-query) \u2014 researcher-added reference level\n\n- **Inputs:** benchmark instance text.\n- **Steps:** (1) construct prompt from `COT_PROMPT_TEMPLATE` (zero-shot, no exemplars, encouraging step-by-step reasoning); (2) issue exactly **one** LLM call; (3) parse the model's free-form answer.\n- **Time components:** LLM call latency only. No retrieval, no execution.\n- **Researcher-supplied:** `COT_PROMPT_TEMPLATE`, `ANSWER_PARSER`.\n\n### 2.2 Expert Prompting (single-query, expert persona)\n\n- **Inputs:** benchmark instance text + expert persona specification.\n- **Steps:** (1) construct prompt from `EXPERT_PROMPT_TEMPLATE` (expert-persona-prefixed single-shot prompt); (2) issue exactly **one** LLM call; (3) parse the model's free-form answer.\n- **Time components:** LLM call latency only.\n- **Researcher-supplied:** `EXPERT_PROMPT_TEMPLATE`, persona specification (domain, role, expertise level).\n\n### 2.3 PAL (Program-Aided Language) \u2014 single-query with code execution\n\n- **Inputs:** benchmark instance text.\n- **Steps:** (1) construct prompt from `PAL_PROMPT_TEMPLATE` (elicits Python code solving the instance); (2) issue exactly **one** LLM call \u2192 response contains code; (3) parse the code block; (4) execute code in `PYTHON_SANDBOX` with timeout `PAL_EXEC_TIMEOUT_S`; (5) from execution output, derive the final answer; (6) on parse or execution failure, record the trial as `SUCCESS = false` without retrying.\n- **Time components:** LLM latency + Python-exec time + parsing/extraction time.\n- **Researcher-supplied:** `PAL_PROMPT_TEMPLATE`, `PYTHON_SANDBOX`, `PAL_EXEC_TIMEOUT_S`.\n\n### 2.4 BoT (Buffer of Thoughts) \u2014 retrieval + template instantiation\n\n- **Inputs:** benchmark instance text.\n- **Steps:** (1) encode input into a retrieval key per the buffer's retrieval protocol (researcher-supplied because no workspace documents are available); (2) retrieve top-`k_TEMPLATES` templates from the pre-built buffer; (3) select a template (researcher-supplied selector); (4) instantiate the template with instance-specific values; (5) optionally make further LLM calls on the instantiated template (number of follow-up calls is method-structural, reported as the (d) secondary component); (6) extract the final answer.\n- **Time components:** LLM-only latency **+** BoT retrieval/instantiation time **+** parsing/extraction time.\n- **Buffer construction:** done **once** per benchmark, off the measurement path for instance latency, with cost amortized at 1/N (item 5).\n- **Researcher-supplied:** retrieval protocol, `k_TEMPLATES`, template selector, instantiation procedure, follow-up call structure, buffer contents.\n\n### 2.5 Operational stack shared across methods\n\n- **Prompt assembly:** pure string concatenation; counted in LLM-call setup for single-query methods, reported separately as `LAT_RETRIEVE` for BoT.\n- **LLM call envelope:** identical HTTP/gRPC client options (retry policy, timeout, connection pooling). Retries disabled \u2014 a retry would be a confound.\n- **Answer extraction:** measured as part of the method's own component (b)/(c); only reported separately if it dominates.\n\n---\n\n## Section 3 \u2014 Benchmarks\n\n### 3.1 Game of 24\n\n- **Task:** given four integers, combine with `+ \u2212 \u00d7 \u00f7` and parentheses to reach 24.\n- **Standard test set size:** commonly 100\u2013256 published instances (researcher-supplied `N_G24`).\n- **Correctness:** success iff the model's (or, for PAL, the executed program's) outcome is exactly 24 and the expression uses each input exactly once.\n\n### 3.2 MGSM (Multilingual Grade-School Math)\n\n- **Task:** word problem, multilingual.\n- **Standard test set:** 250 instances per language, multiple languages available.\n- **Researcher default language selection:** English plus one canonical non-English language (`MGSM_LANGS`).\n- **Correctness:** numeric final answer matches ground truth within published tolerance.\n\n### 3.3 Checkmate-in-One\n\n- **Task:** given a chess position, output a legal move that delivers checkmate on the next ply.\n- **Standard test set:** 1,257 instances; stratified random subsample of \u2265 300 if budget constrained.\n- **Stratification rule:** preserve the published difficulty distribution; document the stratification variable.\n- **Correctness:** the predicted move is legal and mates in one against a responding engine.\n\n### 3.4 Instance-set parameter summary\n\n| Benchmark | Researcher parameter | Default if unspecified |\n|-----------|----------------------|------------------------|\n| Game of 24 | `N_G24`, instance file | Full standard test set (commonly 100\u2013256) |\n| MGSM | `MGSM_LANGS`, instance file | English + 1 canonical non-English, full 250/lang |\n| Checkmate-in-One | `N_CHECKMATE`, instance file | Full 1,257, stratified random subsample \u2265 300 if needed |\n\n---\n\n## Section 4 \u2014 Latency operationalization\n\n### 4.1 Primary metric\n\n`LAT_PRIMARY[i, m, t]` = end-to-end wall-clock seconds, from `t_system_receives_input[i]` to `t_system_returns_final_answer[i, m, t]`, where `i` indexes instance, `m \u2208 {CoT, Expert, PAL, BoT}`, and `t` indexes trial. Single stopwatch-style record at process boundary.\n\n### 4.2 Secondary decomposition\n\nSix auxiliary columns per observation:\n\n1. `LAT_LLM[i, m, t]` \u2014 sum of LLM-call latencies across all calls in trial.\n2. `LAT_RETRIEVE[i, m, t]` \u2014 BoT retrieval + template instantiation (zero for non-BoT).\n3. `LAT_PYEXEC[i, m, t]` \u2014 Python sandbox execution time (zero for non-PAL).\n4. `N_LLM_CALLS[i, m, t]` \u2014 integer count of LLM calls.\n5. `LAT_PARSE[i, m, t]` \u2014 parsing/extraction time (zero for CoT/Expert).\n6. `OVERHEAD[i, m, t]` = `LAT_PRIMARY \u2212 (LAT_LLM + LAT_RETRIEVE + LAT_PYEXEC + LAT_PARSE)` \u2014 residual.\n\n### 4.3 Tertiary proxy\n\n`TOK_TOTAL[i, m, t]` = `input_tokens` + `output_tokens` summed across all LLM calls. Explicitly **not** the answer to the research question; reported to support comparability with prior work that uses tokens instead of seconds.\n\n### 4.4 What is excluded\n\n- One-time buffer construction for BoT (excluded from per-instance latency; amortized at 1/N into a single reporting line per benchmark).\n- Benchmark data loading from disk.\n- Result logging and upload.\n\n---\n\n## Section 5 \u2014 Sampling and trials\n\n### 5.1 Within-subjects design\n\nEvery instance `i` is exercised under every method `m`. This cancels instance-difficulty variance from the method comparison and is the single most important structural choice in the design.\n\n### 5.2 Trial schedule (per item 7)\n\n- Greedy pass: k = 1, temperature = 0.0, deterministic.\n- Stochastic pass: k = 5, temperature = 0.7, top_p = 1.0.\n- Total per (instance, method): 6 trials = 24 trials per instance across the 4 methods.\n\n### 5.3 Greedy vs. stochastic split\n\nThe greedy k = 1 observation is the **headline** per-cell latency number. The stochastic k = 5 observations are pooled (mean, SD) to estimate run-to-run variance. The headline report card uses the greedy point estimate and a \u00b11 SE band from the stochastic SD.\n\n### 5.4 Run-block randomization (per item 11)\n\n- The 4 \u00d7 6 = 24 h time-block scheme applies to the actual execution environment, not the workspace.\n- Within each block: uniform random permutation of all (instance \u00d7 method \u00d7 trial) cells, with the constraint that the same instance is not adjacent in the permutation.\n- A trial-level `RUN_BLOCK` column is recorded for the mixed-effects model.\n\n### 5.5 Failure handling\n\nIf PAL execution times out or raises an exception, the trial is recorded with `SUCCESS = false` and the trial latency includes the timeout duration (the user pays the timeout). The design does **not** retry on failure \u2014 retries would conflate reliability with latency. Failures are reported as a separate aggregate in the analysis.\n\n---\n\n## Section 6 \u2014 Controls\n\n| Confound | Control |\n|----------|---------|\n| Backbone model | Single `BACKBONE_MODEL` pinned to `BACKBONE_VERSION`, identical client options, identical `MAX_TOKENS` (item 2). |\n| Decoding stochasticity | Greedy pass for headline; stochastic k = 5 for variance (item 3). |\n| Prompt length | Approximate length matching across methods where feasible; precise adjustment via `log(prompt_tokens)` covariate (item 12). |\n| PAL Python-exec overhead | Measured as `LAT_PYEXEC` and reported separately (items 1, 4). |\n| BoT retrieval/instantiation overhead | Measured as `LAT_RETRIEVE` and reported separately; one-time buffer build amortized 1/N (items 1, 5). |\n| Server-load variance | Randomization + 4 \u00d7 6 h blocking + `(1|run_block)` random effect (item 11). |\n| Retry storm | Retries disabled at the HTTP client; PAL failures recorded but not retried (item 5.5). |\n| Clock drift | Single monotonic clock source (e.g., `time.monotonic_ns()`). |\n| Order effects | Within-block uniform random permutation of (instance, method, trial) tuples (item 5.4). |\n| Sandbox startup | Per-session startup measured; if significant, amortized across session instances (item 4). |\n\n---\n\n## Section 7 \u2014 Analysis plan\n\n### 7.1 Primary regression (per item 13)\n\n```\nlog(LAT_PRIMARY[i, m, t, b]) \n    ~ method \u00d7 benchmark \n    + log(prompt_tokens[i, m]) \n    + (1 | instance) \n    + (1 | instance:trial) \n    + (1 | run_block)\n```\n\n- `b` indexes run block.\n- Random intercept for `instance` captures instance-difficulty baseline latency; instance is the clustering unit for across-method comparisons.\n- `(1 | instance:trial)` allows the same instance to have different intercepts across trials.\n- `(1 | run_block)` absorbs wall-clock drift in server conditions.\n\n### 7.2 Contrast family (within each benchmark)\n\nBoT vs Expert, BoT vs PAL, BoT vs CoT, PAL vs Expert, PAL vs CoT, Expert vs CoT.\n\nCorrection: Holm\u2013Bonferroni across the family. H1 sets the directional alternative for BoT-vs-single-query contrasts; others are two-sided.\n\n### 7.3 Effect-size reporting\n\n- Estimate: coefficient on `method` (log scale), back-transformed to latency ratio (\"BoT takes \u03b2\u00d7 longer than the reference method\").\n- 95% CI: cluster-bootstrap on instance, 10,000 resamples, stratified by benchmark.\n- Hedges' g on log-latency reported for completeness.\n\n### 7.4 Decomposition models\n\nRe-run the primary regression four times, substituting the dependent variable:\n\n- `log(LAT_LLM)` \u2014 does the PAL gap collapse if Python-exec is removed?\n- `LAT_LLM + LAT_RETRIEVE + LAT_PYEXEC` \u2014 clears inter-call and execution overhead.\n- `N_LLM_CALLS` (Poisson or negative-binomial GLMM) \u2014 does BoT's gap reduce to a call-count gap?\n- `log(LAT_PRIMARY)` with random effects reduced to instance clustering only, as a robustness check.\n\nThese directly test H4.\n\n### 7.5 Accuracy (per item 9)\n\nReported per (method, benchmark) cell as the proportion of trials where `SUCCESS = true`. Not entered as a covariate into the latency model. Reported side by side with latency so the reader can interpret faster-but-worse vs. slower-but-better.\n\n### 7.6 Pre-specified exclusion rules\n\nThe only exclusions permitted, declared in advance:\n\n1. Trials where `BACKBONE_MODEL` returns a refusal (counted, not excluded).\n2. Trials where `LAT_PRIMARY` exceeds `MAX_TRIAL_LATENCY_S` (researcher parameter; default 10\u00d7 median for that cell) \u2014 flagged, not silently dropped.\n3. Pre-block warm-up trials labeled as such.\n\nNo other exclusions. Deviations are documented in the decision log (Section 10).\n\n---\n\n## Section 8 \u2014 Feasibility and budget\n\n### 8.1 Up-front call-count estimate\n\nPer item 7: each (instance, method) cell requires 6 LLM calls. Total:\n\n```\nTOTAL_CALLS = (N_G24 + N_MGSM_PER_LANG \u00d7 |MGSM_LANGS| + N_CHECKMATE) \u00d7 4 \u00d7 6\n```\n\nOrder-of-magnitude under default sizes:\n\n| Benchmark | N | Methods | Trials | Calls |\n|-----------|---|---------|--------|-------|\n| Game of 24 | 256 | 4 | 6 | 6,144 |\n| MGSM (2 langs \u00d7 250) | 500 | 4 | 6 | 12,000 |\n| Checkmate-in-One (full) | 1,257 | 4 | 6 | 30,168 |\n| **Total** | **2,013** | **4** | **6** | **48,312** |\n\nPlus one-off BoT buffer construction (off the measurement path; researcher-supplied iteration count).\n\n### 8.2 Cost projection\n\nPer-call cost depends on `BACKBONE_MODEL`, input/output token counts, and provider pricing. Reported as `TOTAL_CALLS \u00d7 avg_cost_per_call(i, m, trial)` against `BUDGET_USD`. No per-call dollar figure is invented because no model is pinned in this workspace.\n\n### 8.3 Degradation ladder (per item 10)\n\nOn budget pressure, apply in this fixed order:\n\n1. Reduce stochastic `K_STOCHASTIC` from 5 to 3. Saves 2 trials per cell; greedy headline unchanged.\n2. Reduce instances via item-8 fallback (MGSM: single language; Game of 24: shorter published variant; Checkmate-in-One: stratified subsample down to \u2265 300).\n3. Drop the stochastic pass; report greedy only. Removes variance estimation; CIs from cluster bootstrap unavailable.\n4. Drop CoT last. Reduces method count from 4 to 3 (BoT, Expert, PAL \u2014 the literal question's three).\n\nThe rung actually applied is reported alongside results.\n\n### 8.4 Environment capability statement\n\nThe supplied workspace (`bubblewrap` runtime, network disabled, no GPU, system Python only, no preinstalled dependencies) is **execution-incapable for the proposed experiment.** This document is scoped for **an external execution environment** against the budget ceilings in item 10. The design carries no claim of execution in `/workspace` and no latency numbers derived from such execution.\n\n---\n\n## Section 9 \u2014 Threats to validity\n\n1. **Prompt-template variance across methods.** CoT, Expert, PAL, and BoT use structurally different prompts. Even with the `log(prompt_tokens)` covariate, residual prompt-content effects may bias the latency comparison. *Mitigation:* published per-method templates; sensitivity analysis rerun with `log(prompt_tokens)` removed to estimate upper-bound omission bias.\n2. **Backbone-model confound under substitution.** If a single backbone is unavailable for one method (provider constraint), per-method backbone substitution re-introduces model confounds. *Mitigation:* the item-2 default locks the backbone; any substitution is logged as a deviation.\n3. **Server-load variance.** The 4 \u00d7 6 h blocking + random permutation is a partial defense; severe load spikes may survive blocking. *Mitigation:* the `(1|run_block)` random effect absorbs residual drift; sensitivity analysis rerun without it.\n4. **Sample-size limits on Checkmate-in-One.** If the budget ladder reaches rung 2 or 3, fewer Checkmate-in-One observations are available. With stratification the estimate remains representative, but SD inflates. *Mitigation:* power calculation reported; if SD \u00d7 \u221a(1/n) exceeds the smallest BoT-vs-CoT effect size of interest, the experiment is reported as under-powered for that contrast.\n5. **Absence of source documents in the workspace.** This design rests entirely on researcher-decided defaults (Section 0). All method-specific prompts, retrieval selectors, buffer contents, benchmark instance lists, and answer parsers are researcher-supplied parameters because no source material is present in `/workspace`. *Mitigation:* every researcher-supplied parameter is enumerated in Section 10.\n6. **BoT template-match miss-rate interaction with latency.** If the buffer has no template for an instance, BoT either falls back (slow path) or fails; either outcome inflates BoT's measured latency in method-internal ways. *Mitigation:* miss-rate reported per benchmark; secondary decomposition separates LLM-only latency.\n7. **PAL Python-execution timeout conflation.** A timed-out PAL trial costs `PAL_EXEC_TIMEOUT_S` worth of latency; PAL is more likely to time out on harder instances, biasing PAL's estimate upward on those instances. *Mitigation:* timeouts recorded and reported separately; headline latency excludes timed-out trials in a sensitivity analysis.\n8. **MGSM language selection confound.** Choosing one canonical non-English language embeds that language's tokenization cost into the cross-method comparison. *Mitigation:* language enters the mixed model as a fixed effect, or comparators are reported within each language separately.\n9. **Checkmate-in-One parsing overhead.** Move parsing from SAN/FEN is non-trivial; a slow parser would inflate overhead in the wrong component. *Mitigation:* parser is benchmark-internal (shared by all methods) and cancels in the within-subjects comparison.\n10. **Token count as a \"proxy.\"** Token count and wall-clock correlate but are not identical; Python execution has zero tokens. The design carries an explicit caveat that token count cannot answer the research question.\n\n---\n\n## Section 10 \u2014 Decision log: mapping items 1\u201313 to their resolution\n\n| Item | Resolution status |\n|------|-------------------|\n| (1) Inference time | **Resolved.** End-to-end wall-clock primary; six-component secondary; token count tertiary (caveated). |\n| (2) Backbone LLM | **Parameterized.** `BACKBONE_MODEL`, `BACKBONE_PROVIDER`, `BACKBONE_VERSION` researcher-supplied. |\n| (3) Decoding | **Resolved.** Greedy (k=1, T=0.0) + stochastic (k=5, T=0.7, top_p=1.0); `MAX_TOKENS` researcher parameter. |\n| (4) PAL Python execution | **Resolved.** Included in primary; secondary component (c); sandbox type and startup are researcher parameters. |\n| (5) BoT retrieval/instantiation | **Resolved.** Included in primary; secondary component (b); buffer build amortized 1/N per exercising instance. |\n| (6) CoT baseline | **Resolved.** CoT included as 4th level; documented as researcher extension beyond the literal three methods. |\n| (7) Trials per cell | **Resolved.** 6 trials per (instance, method); 24 per instance. |\n| (8) Sample sizes | **Resolved with parameters.** Default conventions documented; `N_G24`, `N_MGSM_PER_LANG`, `MGSM_LANGS`, `N_CHECKMATE` researcher-supplied. |\n| (9) Accuracy | **Resolved.** Secondary descriptive, reported alongside latency, not used to gate. |\n| (10) Budget | **Parameterized.** `BUDGET_USD`, `BUDGET_HOURS`, `BUDGET_MAX_CALLS`; degradation ladder fixed in order (i)\u2013(iv). |\n| (11) Run-block randomization | **Resolved.** 4 \u00d7 6 h blocks, full permutation within blocks, `(1|run_block)` random effect. |\n| (12) Prompt templates | **Parameterized.** Per-method published template strings sourced by researcher; `log(prompt_tokens)` covariate. |\n| (13) Statistical unit and model | **Resolved.** Per-instance per-trial; mixed-effects formula given; contrasts and Holm correction given; bootstrap CIs on instance. |\n\n**Items remaining agnostic to a specific value the researcher must supply (workspace does not provide):**\n\n- `BACKBONE_MODEL`, `BACKBONE_PROVIDER`, `BACKBONE_VERSION` (item 2)\n- `MAX_TOKENS` (item 3)\n- `PAL_PROMPT_TEMPLATE`, `PYTHON_SANDBOX` type, `PAL_EXEC_TIMEOUT_S` (item 4)\n- BoT retrieval protocol, `k_TEMPLATES`, template selector, instantiation procedure, follow-up call structure (item 2.4)\n- `N_G24`, `N_MGSM_PER_LANG`, `MGSM_LANGS`, `N_CHECKMATE`, instance file paths (item 8)\n- `COT_PROMPT_TEMPLATE`, `EXPERT_PROMPT_TEMPLATE` (items 2.1, 2.2)\n- `BUDGET_USD`, `BUDGET_HOURS`, `BUDGET_MAX_CALLS` (item 10)\n\n**Fixed by the supplied `task.json`:** comparator set {BoT, Expert Prompting, PAL} and benchmark set {Game of 24, MGSM, Checkmate-in-One}. CoT added per item 6 with explicit documentation.\n\n---\n\n*End of design document. No experiment is claimed to have been executed. No latency numbers are reported. The supplied workspace (network disabled, no GPU, system Python only, no preinstalled deps) is acknowledged as execution-incapable; this design is scoped for an external execution environment against the researcher-supplied budget.*", "evidence": "The full design is built on the Section 0 defaults exactly as specified. Section 1 frames five hypotheses (H1-H5) as researcher-stated predictions, with the contrast family and Holm-Bonferroni correction pre-specified. Section 2 operationalizes BoT, Expert, PAL, and CoT as design-level templates with researcher-supplied parameters where specifics are absent from the workspace; no prompt-text, retrieval key construction, or buffer contents are fabricated. Section 3 parameterizes the three benchmark instance sets and the degradation subsample rules, using the conventional N values cited in the task (100-256 / 250 per language / 1,257 with >= 300 stratified fallback). Section 4 carries the primary/secondary/tertiary latency decomposition with an explicit caveat that token count is not the answer. Section 5 specifies within-subjects over methods with the k=1 greedy plus k=5 stochastic schedule and 4x6h run-block randomization. Section 6 enumerates ten controls. Section 7 specifies the mixed-effects model formula, contrast family, bootstrap CIs, decomposition models, and accuracy treatment. Section 8 reports the call-count estimate under default sizes (48,312 total) and a four-rung degradation ladder, and states that the bubblewrap workspace is execution-incapable. Section 9 lists ten threats to validity including prompt-template variance, backbone substitution, server load, sample-size limits, absence of workspace sources, BoT miss-rate, PAL timeouts, MGSM language, parsing overhead, and the token-count proxy caveat. Section 10 maps items 1-13 to resolution status and lists each remaining researcher-supplied parameter. No aspect of the design invents a latency number, claims execution, or relies on source documents not present in /workspace.", "assumptions": ["Adopted from the researcher's binding defaults in turn: end-to-end wall-clock is primary; token count is explicitly not the answer; one pinned backbone parameterized by BACKBONE_MODEL/BACKBONE_PROVIDER/BACKBONE_VERSION; greedy k=1 plus stochastic k=5; PAL Python execution included in primary and isolated as component (c); BoT retrieval/instantiation included in primary, isolated as component (b), buffer build amortized 1/N; CoT added as 4th level and explicitly labeled researcher extension; 6 trials per (instance, method) and 24 per instance; full published test sets with stratified subsample fallback rules, exact N values parameterized; accuracy secondary descriptive; budget parameterized with degradation ladder in fixed order (i)-(iv); 4x6 h run-block randomization; prompts from original papers parameterized with log(prompt_tokens) covariate; per-instance per-trial unit, mixed-effects model with (1|instance), (1|instance:trial), (1|run_block), Holm correction, cluster-bootstrap CIs on instance.", "Workspace contains only /workspace/task.json. No track.md file exists on disk. No source papers, prompt templates, code, or benchmark instance files are present in the workspace; all such specifics are researcher-supplied parameters listed in Section 10.", "The supplied bubblewrap environment is execution-incapable and no execution is claimed or attempted.", "All latency numbers, accuracy numbers, call costs, and per-call dollar figures are NOT estimated in the document; placeholders are used throughout where a fixed value is required."], "uncertainties": ["Backbone LLM identity, version pin, and provider are researcher-supplied and not in the workspace; the design parameterizes these rather than picking values.", "Per-method prompt template contents and exemplars are researcher-supplied; the design treats them as audit-able inputs rather than baked-in.", "BoT internal specifics (retrieval key construction, template-scoring selector, follow-up call count, buffer contents) are researcher-supplied because no source documents exist in /workspace.", "Per-benchmark N values and the MGSM non-English language choice are researcher-supplied; the design gives the conventional defaults but does not pin them.", "Budget ceilings (USD, hours, max calls) are researcher-supplied; the degradation ladder handles all plausible budget pressures in a fixed order.", "Item 6 (CoT addition) is a researcher extension beyond the literal three methods in the question; the deviation is documented in Section 0, Section 2, and Section 10.", "Whether the mixed-effects analysis package will be available in the execution environment is itself a researcher decision; a fixed-effects cluster-robust SE fallback is documented in Section 0 under (13).", "The 'track.md' entry in task.json is the only reference to that name; no actual file exists on disk and the design treats it as a one-line embedded instruction.", "The exact sample-size defaults (256 for Game of 24, 1,257 for Checkmate-in-One, 250 per MGSM language) are conventional values cited by the researcher's defaults block rather than verified-from-workspace values; the design labels them as defaults and parameterizes the precise N."]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-c326bf13-efb8-4bc8-8535-07d1f7b9916e", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 19}
